  "body_text": null,
  "visible_text": "{\"count\":3,\"rationale\":\"Block memoryDump:credentialAccess, fileServer:lateralMovement and codeRepo:collection.\\n\\nWhy these 3 work:\\n1. Blocking memoryDump makes dbLogin unreachable (it is AND with memoryDump). Without dbLogin, localAdmin, hypervisor and secretsManager are all unreachable, so vaultRead is dead.\\n2. Blocking memoryDump also kills apiToken (AND with memoryDump). That removes backupHost and serviceAccount.\\n3. Blocking fileServer then leaves shareMount with no live parent (fileServer, backupHost, hypervisor all dead). dbReplica is dead for the same reason (serviceAccount, fileServer, backupHost all dead).\\n4. codeRepo is blocked directly.\\nAll four parents of crownData are now unreachable, so the target is unreachable.\\n\\nWhy 2 is not enough:\\n- shareMount and dbReplica both have fileServer as an OR parent. fileServer stays reachable through sshKey (from exposedApi, an entry) or ssoLogin (from phishing-reachable steps). So it costs one block: either fileServer itself, or both shareMount and dbReplica. If you spend both blocks on shareMount and dbReplica, vaultRead and codeRepo remain open.\\n- So one block goes to fileServer. The single remaining block cannot cover both codeRepo and vaultRead. codeRepo stays reachable through printServer, which is fed by kerberoast from the entries. Blocking codeRepo itself leaves vaultRead reachable.\"}",
